# +Point, -Surface
#   * <module> The set-free series: an identity that holds only on data
#
# A family of the set-free series on reverse eta at data, which is a premise
# with its proof, and which grammar compression must therefore support.
# A point is a closed pure lambda program, no set in it, whose answer is one
# binary numeral.
#
# THE PROGRAM applies a function n times to a binary numeral x:
# repeat n f x = n x (\p. f (repeat p f x)), n a unary numeral, repeat tied by
# the prelude's fixpoint. f is written by the program itself, not taken from
# the attractor library, so that recognising a library function does not
# answer it:
# - REBUILD: f takes a numeral apart by its constructors and builds it again,
#   zero to zero, even h to even (f h), odd h to odd (f h). On every binary
#   numeral f is the identity, by induction on the numeral; on a term that is
#   no numeral it is not (f (\x. x) is no \x. x). So f = \x. x holds under the
#   premise that its argument is a numeral, the data eta of the typing
#   plan's first step, and with it the program is x. Without it, weak_head
#   rebuilds x n times as the decoding reads it, about n times its bits.
# - NEAR MISS, a proved trap for simplification by shape: f exchanges the
#   even and odd constructors at every level, the complement of every bit.
#   It is no identity: with n odd the program's normal form is the
#   complement of x, and an answer x is outside S(P).
# - INVOLUTION: f is the complement, applied an even number of times. The
#   complement composed with itself is the identity on every binary numeral
#   (by induction on the numeral) and not on other terms, so the program is x
#   under the premise that x is a numeral and the lemma that the complement
#   is an involution. The rebuild's near miss, the complement applied an odd
#   number of times, is its control.
# - UNIT LAW: f is \acc. plus acc zero, plus the attractor merge_binary_plus.
#   plus acc zero reduces to acc on every canonical binary numeral (by
#   induction on acc: 0 + 0 = 0, 2h + 0 = 2(h + 0), 2h + 1 + 0 = 2(h + 0) + 1)
#   and not on other terms, so the program is x under the same premise. Its
#   NEAR MISS is \acc. plus acc one, whose program is x + n.
# The involution and the unit law were added on 2026-09-30 (the design
# document, family 4).
# Each point has a DIRECT and a RESPELLED twin
# (contest_attractors:spelled_surface/3).
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - positive supercompilation, which propagates the constructor a variable
#   was matched against into the branch and folds a repeated configuration:
#   M. H. Sørensen, R. Glück, N. D. Jones, A Positive Supercompiler, J.
#   Funct. Program. 1996, doi:10.1017/S0956796800002008;
# - proving the equivalence of two terms, here f and the identity on
#   numerals, by supercompiling both: I. Klyuchnikov, S. Romanenko, Proving
#   the Equivalence of Higher-Order Terms by Means of Supercompilation,
#   PSI 2009, LNCS 2010, doi:10.1007/978-3-642-11486-1_17;
# - finding and proving inductive lemmas about recursive functions on
#   inductive data: K. Claessen, M. Johansson, D. Rosén, N. Smallbone,
#   Automating Inductive Proofs Using Theory Exploration, CADE 2013,
#   doi:10.1007/978-3-642-38574-2_27; E. Singher, S. Itzhaky, Theory
#   Exploration Powered by Deductive Synthesis, CAV 2021,
#   doi:10.1007/978-3-030-81688-9_6.
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the tree of x for a rebuild, an involution and a unit law
# point, the complement of x's tree for the rebuild's near miss (n odd), and
# the tree of x + n for the unit law's near miss. PROOF. The program is pure, so S(P) = {N} for
# its normal form N. repeat n f x reduces to
# f (f (... (f x))), n applications, by induction on n. By induction on the
# numeral, the rebuild f applied to a canonical numeral reduces to that
# numeral, and the complement to the numeral with every even and odd
# exchanged; the complement is an involution, so n odd applications give
# the complement once. Both are Scott numerals, normal forms; the complement
# of x differs from x wherever x has a bit, so the near miss's normal form
# is not x. The involution's n even applications give x, and the
# unit law's n applications of plus acc zero give x by the induction above;
# its near miss gives x + n by n applications of plus acc one on canonical
# numerals (merge_binary_plus is binary addition, the attractor's reference).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   rebuild_point(?Point, ?Function, ?Spelling, ?Times, ?Number)
#
# The spelling is a constructor of schema/spelling, direct or respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "spelling")
λ direct respelled
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_rebuild_small_direct") (text "rebuild") direct (integer 3) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_rebuild_small_respelled") (text "rebuild") respelled (integer 3) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_rebuild_small_near_miss") (text "complement") direct (integer 3) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_involution_small_direct") (text "complement") direct (integer 4) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point
      (text "set_free_involution_small_respelled")
      (text "complement") respelled
      (integer 4) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_unit_small_direct") (text "unit") direct (integer 3) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _rebuild_point
    . _rebuild_point (text "set_free_unit_small_respelled") (text "unit") respelled (integer 3) λ. integer 45
  )
λ
